    "# Fake or Real: The Impostor Hunt in Texts\n",
    "\n",
    "Approach with **Pairwise Ranking Prompting (PRP)**, which is a technique where you prompt a large language model (LLM) to compare two texts and decide which one better matches a given criterion (e.g., which is more \"real,\" more relevant, or preferred).\n",
    "\n",
    "**Pairwise prompt:**\n",
    "```\n",
    "prompt = f\"\"\"Which of the following two passages is more real?\n",
    "\n",
    "Passage A:\n",
    "{text1}\n",
    "\n",
    "Passage B:\n",
    "{text2}\n",
    "\n",
    "Answer with \"Passage A\" or \"Passage B\" only.\"\"\"\n",
    "response = llm(prompt)[0]['generated_text']\n",
    "```\n",
    "\n",
    "**PRP unit:**\n",
    "![image.png](attachment:0199abe2-fd85-4103-a9fa-7363bf518a84.png)\n",
    "Jian Luo, Xuanang Chen, Ben He, and Le Sun. 2024. PRP-Graph: Pairwise Ranking Prompting to LLMs with Graph Aggregation for Effective Text Re-ranking. In _Proceedings of the 62nd Annual Meeting of the Association for Computational Linguistics (Volume 1: Long Papers)_, pages 5766–5776, Bangkok, Thailand. Association for Computational Linguistics.\n",
    "\n",
    "I will also use **prompt engineering** by including representative examples from the training data directly in the prompt (few-shot learning). For pairwise ranking, this means showing the model several example pairs and the correct choice before presenting the actual pair to be ranked.\n"
   ]
